{
  "generated_text": "To determine the length of the belt that is tightly drawn around three circles of radius \\(10\\) cm each, we need to consider both the straight segments and the arc segments of the belt.\n\n### Step 1: Calculate the straight segments\nEach circle has a radius of \\(10\\) cm, so the diameter of each circle is:\n\\[\n2 \\times 10 = 20 \\text{ cm}\n\\]\nSince the belt is drawn tightly around three circles, it will pass through the gaps between the circles. The centers of the three circles form an equilateral triangle with each side equal to the diameter of one circle, which is \\(20\\) cm.\n\nThe distance between the centers of any two circles is \\(20\\) cm. The belt will form straight segments along the lines connecting the centers of the circles. There are three such straight segments, each of length \\(20\\) cm. Therefore, the total length of the straight segments is:\n\\[\n3 \\times 20 = 60 \\text{ cm}\n\\]\n\n### Step 2: Calculate the arc segments\nThe belt also forms arcs around the circles. Each arc is a quarter-circle (since the belt passes through the gaps between the circles, forming a \\(90^\\circ\\) or \\(\\frac{1}{4}\\) of a full circle).\n\nEach quarter-circle has a radius of \\(10\\) cm. The length of one quarter-circle is:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{4} \\times 2\\pi \\times 10 = \\frac{1}{4} \\times 20\\pi = 5\\pi \\text{ cm}\n\\]\nSince there are three such quarter-circles, the total length of the arc segments is:\n\\[\n3 \\times 5\\pi = 15\\pi \\text{ cm}\n\\]\n\n### Step 3: Calculate the total length of the belt\nAdding the lengths of the straight segments and the arc segments, we get:\n\\[\n60 + 15\\pi \\text{ cm}\n\\]\n\n### Step 4: Identify \\(a\\) and \\(b\\)\nFrom the expression \\(60 + 15\\pi\\), we identify:\n\\[\na = 60 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad b = 15\n\\]\n\n### Step 5: Calculate \\(a + b\\)\n\\[\na + b = 60 + 15 = 75\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(a + b\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{75}\n\\]",
